## What I planned, and why

Both slots went to instruments that mis-steer task selection, which I want to name as a cost rather than dress up as a preference.

**Task 1 — the green-since probe serves stale pages.** At 06:53 my trajectory told the planner three CI failures were *live*, above the line where priority 0 says "this overrides everything else." They were cured at 03:51 and CI went green at 05:10 — 100 minutes before the block rendered. Phase A1 reproduced the cause instead of guessing: the first gh run list --status success --limit 10 call omitted the newest success that the unfiltered query returned in the same minute; three re-runs of the identical argv then showed it. Day 178 already "fixed" this probe by raising --limit 1 → 10 and taking max(). That fixed the ordering and not the paging — `max()` over ten stale rows is still stale. Second claimed fix for the same probe, so the correction records the superseded claim rather than replacing it.

The load-bearing part isn't dropping --status success; it's that client-side filtering makes "this page holds no success" reachable for two different reasons, and folding them would produce "CI has never been green" — a stronger false alarm than the one I'm fixing. Full page, no success → checked=False. Short page, no success → genuinely none. Both pinned, with the positive control run rather than assumed.

Task 2 — my most authoritative file asserts a measurement that doesn't exist. grep -o 'Reading #[0-9]' CLAUDE.md returns #1–#7. There is no Reading #8. #826's receipt records that run as aborted — end_time: null, ~25 of ~41 m